# G065 — Progress Bar for Downloads

*v2. See `progress-bar.md` for prose: producer and consumer as distinct
actors, atomics as minimum synchronisation, completion as state not
flag, cooperative cancellation, consumer chooses its own cadence,
time as first-class input.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Long-Running Agent Task with Live Progress

[long-fetch @url @dest
    IN ORDER TO
        stream a large file while a separate display shows live progress
        and let the user cancel mid-transfer without killing the worker

    @total   <- [http[content-length {url: @url}]]
    @tracker <- [progress[new {total: @total}]]

    concurrent [
        worker:
            for @chunk in [http[stream-chunks {url: @url}]]
                @tracker
                    where is-cancelled is true -> break
                [fs[append {path: @dest, bytes: @chunk}]]
                [progress[advance
                            {tracker: @tracker, delta: @chunk:size}]]
            [progress[complete {tracker: @tracker}]]

        display:
            every 100 ms
                @tracker where status is not "running" -> break
                [ui[render-bar
                      {snapshot: [progress[snapshot
                                             {tracker: @tracker}]]}]]
    ]
    join

    sync @user-events {
        cancel -> [progress[cancel {tracker: @tracker}]]
                  ;; worker sees flag on next chunk
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[progress
    new             {total}
    advance         {tracker, delta}             ;; producer thread
    complete        {tracker}
    cancel          {tracker}                     ;; cooperative
    is-cancelled    {tracker}
    snapshot        {tracker}                     ;; consistent read
    status          {tracker}                     ;; "running"|"completed"|"cancelled"|"failed"
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: cooperative cancellation as `where`-checkpoint

[progress[advance {tracker: @t, delta: @d}]
    IN ORDER TO
        keep forward progress under concurrent cancellation signals
        without preemptively killing the worker thread (which would
        leak locks, files, and half-written buffers)

    @t
        where status is "running"
            -> atomically add @d to bytes-done
        where status is terminal (Completed | Cancelled | Failed)
            -> no-op (terminal is sticky via compare-exchange)
]

Preemptive kill is unsafe in almost every runtime. The worker checks
a flag between chunks; the granularity of the chunk IS the
cancellation latency. Too-coarse = slow response; too-fine = overhead.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: consumer cadence `where` independent of producer rate

[every 100 ms
    @snap <- [progress[snapshot {tracker: @t}]]
    [ui[render-bar {snap: @snap}]]
]

The producer may write millions of increments per second; the
consumer polls at display rate. Coalescing is explicit: consumer
picks cadence, atomic snapshot guarantees consistent multi-field view.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @t <- [progress[new {total: 1000}]]

    concurrent [
        for @chunk in [0..1000] stepped by 100
            [progress[advance {tracker: @t, delta: 100}]]
            [progress[complete {tracker: @t}]]
    |
        every 50 ms until @t:status is not "running"
            -> [progress[snapshot {tracker: @t}]]
    ]
    join

    -> [progress[status {tracker: @t}]]     ;; -> "completed"
    -> [progress[snapshot {tracker: @t}]]    ;; -> {bytes-done: 1000, total: 1000, ...}
]
